fields scale displayed token amounts;

395
00:21:14,042 --> 00:21:17,702
Chad G. P. Taylor: these distribution
parameters don't all use that widget. Keep

396
00:21:17,702 --> 00:21:21,363
Chad G. P. Taylor: the units overlay
visible as we change functions. A

397
00:21:21,363 --> 00:21:24,617
Chad G. P. Taylor: familiar-looking number
can represent a different amount here.

398
00:21:24,633 --> 00:21:28,175
Chad G. P. Taylor: The Function selector
determines the amount for each period.

399
00:21:28,175 --> 00:21:31,322
Chad G. P. Taylor: Information buttons
explain formulas, and our tables show

400
00:21:31,322 --> 00:21:33,683
Chad G. P. Taylor: small worked examples
in base units.

401
00:21:33,883 --> 00:21:37,811
Ewa Dashwood: Can I treat minimum,
maximum, and trailing amount as

402
00:21:37,811 --> 00:21:38,683
Ewa Dashwood: interchangeable safeguards?

403
00:21:38,883 --> 00:21:42,724
Chad G. P. Taylor: No, each changes a
particular part of the calculation. We'll

404
00:21:42,724 --> 00:21:46,181
Chad G. P. Taylor: name every parameter we
enter rather than copying a

405
00:21:46,181 --> 00:21:47,333
Chad G. P. Taylor: curve by appearance.

406
00:21:47,533 --> 00:21:51,283
Ewa Dashwood: And Start Period Offset
means a delay after registration?

407
00:21:51,483 --> 00:21:56,267
Chad G. P. Taylor: Don't read it that way.
It isn't simply a number of days to wait.

408
00:21:56,267 --> 00:21:59,342
Chad G. P. Taylor: We'll leave the
optional start field empty in these

409
00:21:59,342 --> 00:22:01,733
Chad G. P. Taylor: recipes and use this
build's precise interpretation.

410
00:22:01,733 --> 00:22:06,533
Ewa Dashwood: Our table begins at k equals
zero. Is that an extra allocation

411
00:22:06,533 --> 00:22:07,333
Ewa Dashwood: at registration?

412
00:22:07,533 --> 00:22:11,639
Chad G. P. Taylor: No. Initial Supply is
separate. Here k measures the period index

413
00:22:11,639 --> 00:22:14,998
Chad G. P. Taylor: from the registration
period, with the optional start field

414
00:22:14,998 --> 00:22:17,983
Chad G. P. Taylor: empty. We include zero
to illustrate the formula.

415
00:22:18,183 --> 00:22:21,533
Ewa Dashwood: Which values contribute over
five eligible periods?

416
00:22:21,733 --> 00:22:24,909
Chad G. P. Taylor: One through five,
excluding the starting boundary and

417
00:22:24,909 --> 00:22:28,085
Chad G. P. Taylor: including the end.
Network period indices determine those

418
00:22:28,085 --> 00:22:31,261
Chad G. P. Taylor: boundaries. Don't
interpret the table as a stopwatch

419
00:22:31,261 --> 00:22:34,833
Chad G. P. Taylor: promising a first claim
exactly twenty-four hours after Confirm.

420
00:22:35,033 --> 00:22:39,247
Ewa Dashwood: So these are calculation
examples, not five payments we've

421
00:22:39,247 --> 00:22:40,183
Ewa Dashwood: already made.

422
00:22:40,383 --> 00:22:43,483
Chad G. P. Taylor: Exactly. No claim is
being performed here.

423
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:47,687
Chad G. P. Taylor: Choose FixedAmount and
enter one hundred in Fixed Amount per

424
00:22:47,687 --> 00:22:51,875
Chad G. P. Taylor: Interval. Each period
contributes one hundred base units, or one

425
00:22:51,875 --> 00:22:53,550
Chad G. P. Taylor: token at two decimals.

426
00:22:53,750 --> 00:22:58,577
Ewa Dashwood: Five eligible periods would
contribute five hundred base units:

427
00:22:58,577 --> 00:22:59,650
Ewa Dashwood: five tokens?

428
00:22:59,850 --> 00:23:03,191
Chad G. P. Taylor: Before other applicable
restrictions, yes. A fixed recurring

429
00:23:03,191 --> 00:23:06,950
Chad G. P. Taylor: treasury allowance is a
straightforward use for this recipe.

430
00:23:07,150 --> 00:23:10,600
Ewa Dashwood: Does fixed mean the total
token supply is fixed too?

431
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,642
Chad G. P. Taylor: Only the recurring
amount is fixed here. Supply limits and

432
00:23:14,642 --> 00:23:16,947
Chad G. P. Taylor: permission rules are
separate settings, and

433
00:23:16,947 --> 00:23:18,100
Chad G. P. Taylor: Claim still matters.

434
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:22,050
Ewa Dashwood: How would we taper a reward
toward a small continuing allowance?

435
00:23:22,250 --> 00:23:25,950
Chad G. P. Taylor: Choose StepDecreasing.
Set Step Count two, Decrease Numerator

436
00:23:25,950 --> 00:23:30,112
Chad G. P. Taylor: one, and Denominator
two. That fraction is removed each

437
00:23:30,112 --> 00:23:34,275
Chad G. P. Taylor: reduction, leaving
half. Leave the start field empty; use

438
00:23:34,275 --> 00:23:37,975
Chad G. P. Taylor: initial amount one
hundred, minimum ten, maximum interval

439
00:23:37,975 --> 00:23:40,750
Chad G. P. Taylor: count three, and
trailing amount ten.

440
00:23:40,950 --> 00:23:43,050
Ewa Dashwood: What sequence should the
table show?

441
00:23:43,250 --> 00:23:46,917
Chad G. P. Taylor: One hundred twice,
fifty twice, twenty-five twice, twelve

442
00:23:46,917 --> 00:23:50,583
Chad G. P. Taylor: twice, then ten.
Maximum Interval Count limits reduction

443
00:23:50,583 --> 00:23:54,708
Chad G. P. Taylor: eras, not individual
days. The trailing branch begins after

444
00:23:54,708 --> 00:23:57,000
Chad G. P. Taylor: that era count is
exceeded.

445
00:23:57,200 --> 00:24:00,400
Ewa Dashwood: Does the minimum
automatically raise the trailing amount?

446
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:03,900
Chad G. P. Taylor: No. The trailing value
is a separate choice.

447
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:08,692
Chad G. P. Taylor: Stepwise gives us an
explicit table. Ensure there are exactly

448
00:24:08,692 --> 00:24:13,483
Chad G. P. Taylor: three rows: start zero,
amount ten; start three, amount five;

449
00:24:13,483 --> 00:24:15,400
Chad G. P. Taylor: start five, amount
zero.

450
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:19,250
Ewa Dashwood: So the latest start we've
reached supplies the amount?

451
00:24:19,450 --> 00:24:24,993
Chad G. P. Taylor: Yes: ten through index
two, five at three and four, then zero.

452
00:24:24,993 --> 00:24:28,226
Chad G. P. Taylor: That stops future
emissions; it doesn't erase

453
00:24:28,226 --> 00:24:29,150
Chad G. P. Taylor: existing balances.

454
00:24:29,350 --> 00:24:33,200
Ewa Dashwood: Can we add two rows with the
same start to combine rewards?

455
00:24:33,400 --> 00:24:36,775
Chad G. P. Taylor: A duplicate index
replaces an earlier value rather than

456
00:24:36,775 --> 00:24:40,900
Chad G. P. Taylor: adding another payment.
Use at least two distinct starts. Add Step

457
00:24:40,900 --> 00:24:45,025
Chad G. P. Taylor: and Remove edit draft
rows; we'll briefly add and remove an

458
00:24:45,025 --> 00:24:48,025
Chad G. P. Taylor: extra practice row.
This suits a campaign with

459
00:24:48,025 --> 00:24:49,150
Chad G. P. Taylor: distinct reward phases.

460
00:24:49,167 --> 00:24:52,427
Ewa Dashwood: For a reward rising by the
same amount each period, should

461
00:24:52,427 --> 00:24:53,317
Ewa Dashwood: I choose Linear?

462
00:24:53,517 --> 00:24:57,917
Chad G. P. Taylor: Yes. Set slope
numerator a to two, divisor d to one,

463
00:24:57,917 --> 00:25:03,117
Chad G. P. Taylor: leave s empty, and set
starting amount b to ten. Use minimum zero

464
00:25:03,117 --> 00:25:04,317
Chad G. P. Taylor: and maximum twenty.

465
00:25:04,517 --> 00:25:10,029
Ewa Dashwood: That gives ten, twelve,
fourteen, sixteen, eighteen, twenty, then

466
00:25:10,029 --> 00:25:11,867
Ewa Dashwood: stays at twenty?

467
00:25:12,067 --> 00:25:15,478
Chad G. P. Taylor: Correct. The positive
divisor controls the slope's scale.

468
00:25:15,478 --> 00:25:19,317
Chad G. P. Taylor: Negative slopes can
model a decline, with deliberate bounds.

469
00:25:19,517 --> 00:25:22,217
Ewa Dashwood: Is maximum twenty our token
supply limit?

470
00:25:22,417 --> 00:25:26,467
Chad G. P. Taylor: It's the function's
per-period maximum. Max Supply is the

471
00:25:26,467 --> 00:25:29,167
Chad G. P. Taylor: separate ceiling on
outstanding token supply.

472
00:25:29,167 --> 00:25:35,205
Chad G. P. Taylor: Polynomial supports a
power curve. Enter a one, m two, n one, d

473
00:25:35,205 --> 00:25:40,780
Chad G. P. Taylor: one; leave s empty. Set
argument offset o one, added amount b

474
00:25:40,780 --> 00:25:43,567
Chad G. P. Taylor: zero, minimum zero,
maximum one hundred.

475
00:25:43,767 --> 00:25:49,017
Ewa Dashwood: With m divided by n as the
exponent, that's k plus one, squared?

476
00:25:49,217 --> 00:25:53,537
Chad G. P. Taylor: Yes: one, four, nine,
sixteen, twenty-five, thirty-six. The

477
00:25:53,537 --> 00:25:58,937
Chad G. P. Taylor: offset changes the
input inside the power; b adds to

478
00:25:58,937 --> 00:26:00,017
Chad G. P. Taylor: the result.

479
00:26:00,217 --> 00:26:03,467
Ewa Dashwood: What if the exponent is
fractional, or just one?

480
00:26:03,667 --> 00:26:07,294
Chad G. P. Taylor: Fractional powers
involve rounding. A positive exponent of

481
00:26:07,294 --> 00:26:10,922
Chad G. P. Taylor: one belongs in Linear.
Use accelerating rewards only

482
00:26:10,922 --> 00:26:15,003
Chad G. P. Taylor: deliberately, with
their cost bounded; the curve doesn't make

483
00:26:15,003 --> 00:26:16,817
Chad G. P. Taylor: that decision for us.

484
00:26:16,833 --> 00:26:19,483
Ewa Dashwood: Does Exponential use powers
of two?

485
00:26:19,683 --> 00:26:23,469
Chad G. P. Taylor: The natural exponential
uses base e. It can also describe decline.

486
00:26:23,469 --> 00:26:29,319
Chad G. P. Taylor: Set a ten, m one, n
one, d one; leave s empty. Set o and b to

487
00:26:29,319 --> 00:26:31,383
Chad G. P. Taylor: zero, minimum zero,
maximum one hundred.

488
00:26:31,583 --> 00:26:33,083
Ewa Dashwood: What does that produce?

489
00:26:33,283 --> 00:26:36,706
Chad G. P. Taylor: Ten, twenty-seven,
seventy-three, then one hundred as the

490
00:26:36,706 --> 00:26:40,983
Chad G. P. Taylor: bound takes effect. The
m-over-n ratio scales the exponent; d

491
00:26:40,983 --> 00:26:46,117
Chad G. P. Taylor: divides the result, o
shifts its input, and b adds an amount.

492
00:26:46,117 --> 00:26:48,683
Chad G. P. Taylor: Positive growth
requires a maximum here.

493
00:26:48,883 --> 00:26:51,783
Ewa Dashwood: Much faster growth than our
linear example.

494
00:26:51,983 --> 00:26:55,868
Chad G. P. Taylor: Exactly. I enjoy an
elegant curve, but my enthusiasm isn't

495
00:26:55,868 --> 00:26:57,033
Chad G. P. Taylor: an emission budget.

496
00:26:57,033 --> 00:27:00,033
Ewa Dashwood: Can we keep increasing a
reward while slowing its growth?

497
00:27:00,233 --> 00:27:05,883
Chad G. P. Taylor: Logarithmic can do
that. Set a ten, d one, m one, n one;

498
00:27:05,883 --> 00:27:10,664
Chad G. P. Taylor: leave s empty. Use o
one, b ten, minimum zero, maximum

499
00:27:10,664 --> 00:27:11,533
Chad G. P. Taylor: one hundred.

500
00:27:11,733 --> 00:27:14,033
Ewa Dashwood: What happens to the first
outputs?

501
00:27:14,233 --> 00:27:17,642
Chad G. P. Taylor: Ten, sixteen, twenty,
twenty-three, twenty-six, twenty-seven.

502
00:27:17,642 --> 00:27:24,461
Chad G. P. Taylor: This uses the natural
logarithm of k plus one. The offset keeps

503
00:27:24,461 --> 00:27:26,733
Chad G. P. Taylor: the starting input
positive.

504
00:27:26,933 --> 00:27:30,783
Ewa Dashwood: Do m and n form a power
exponent here too?

505
00:27:30,983 --> 00:27:33,763
Chad G. P. Taylor: They scale the
logarithm's input, despite the generic

506
00:27:33,763 --> 00:27:36,891
Chad G. P. Taylor: form label. The
logarithmic part is rounded down before

507
00:27:36,891 --> 00:27:37,933
Chad G. P. Taylor: b is added.

508
00:27:37,933 --> 00:27:41,133
Chad G. P. Taylor: InvertedLogarithmic
takes the logarithm of a reciprocal. Keep

509
00:27:41,133 --> 00:27:47,533
Chad G. P. Taylor: a ten, d one, m one, n
one, s empty, o one; change b to forty,

510
00:27:47,533 --> 00:27:50,333
Chad G. P. Taylor: with minimum zero and
maximum one hundred.

511
00:27:50,533 --> 00:27:52,483
Ewa Dashwood: What values should we see?

512
00:27:52,683 --> 00:27:55,948
Chad G. P. Taylor: Forty, thirty-three,
twenty-nine, twenty-six, twenty-three,

513
00:27:55,948 --> 00:28:01,172
Chad G. P. Taylor: twenty-two. Rounding
the negative logarithmic part down gives

514
00:28:01,172 --> 00:28:03,783
Chad G. P. Taylor: thirty-three rather
than thirty-four.

515
00:28:03,983 --> 00:28:06,933
Ewa Dashwood: Is the formula one divided
by a logarithm?

516
00:28:07,133 --> 00:28:11,739
Chad G. P. Taylor: No. The reciprocal is
inside the logarithm: n above m times the

517
00:28:11,739 --> 00:28:15,577
Chad G. P. Taylor: shifted period. This
example declines toward a zero floor. All

518
00:28:15,577 --> 00:28:18,648
Chad G. P. Taylor: eight functions are
alternatives; selecting one doesn't run

519
00:28:18,648 --> 00:28:20,183
Chad G. P. Taylor: the others alongside
it.

520
00:28:20,200 --> 00:28:22,850
Ewa Dashwood: Which time units are
actually available?

521
00:28:23,050 --> 00:28:27,407
Chad G. P. Taylor: Seconds, minutes,
hours, days, weeks, and years. They are

522
00:28:27,407 --> 00:28:31,764
Chad G. P. Taylor: fixed multipliers, not
a calendar scheduler. There's no Month

523
00:28:31,764 --> 00:28:33,700
Chad G. P. Taylor: option in this build.

524
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:37,100
Ewa Dashwood: Does every selectable
interval pass network validation?

525
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:41,300
Chad G. P. Taylor: No. Supported time
intervals must align to whole minutes; one

526
00:28:41,300 --> 00:28:45,700
Chad G. P. Taylor: second fails. The year
multiplier also makes one year fail that

527
00:28:45,700 --> 00:28:49,300
Chad G. P. Taylor: alignment. We'll use
the verified one-day example. Epoch and

528
00:28:49,300 --> 00:28:51,700
Chad G. P. Taylor: block intervals have
their own constraints.

529
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:55,400
Ewa Dashwood: And the help illustration
follows my current parameters?

530
00:28:55,600 --> 00:29:00,083
Chad G. P. Taylor: It's static explanatory
artwork. An empty optional bound can mean

531
00:29:00,083 --> 00:29:03,670
Chad G. P. Taylor: no bound; zero has
field-specific meaning. Consult the

532
00:29:03,670 --> 00:29:07,705
Chad G. P. Taylor: parameter table for
these and offsets. Random isn't a

533
00:29:07,705 --> 00:29:09,050
Chad G. P. Taylor: selectable function
here.

534
00:29:09,067 --> 00:29:12,682
Ewa Dashwood: Could we allow changes to
the emission formula while separately

535
00:29:12,682 --> 00:29:13,767
Ewa Dashwood: locking its recipient?

536
00:29:13,967 --> 00:29:17,834
Chad G. P. Taylor: This creator offers one
Perpetual Distribution Rules section for

537
00:29:17,834 --> 00:29:20,842
Chad G. P. Taylor: changes to the
recurring configuration, including its

538
00:29:20,842 --> 00:29:24,279
Chad G. P. Taylor: recipient. There isn't
a separate recipient-change rule. Whoever

539
00:29:24,279 --> 00:29:27,717
Chad G. P. Taylor: may change that
configuration can potentially affect both.

540
00:29:27,917 --> 00:29:31,517
Ewa Dashwood: So we should review the
performer, administrator, and three

541
00:29:31,517 --> 00:29:33,517
Ewa Dashwood: switches as a single policy.

542
00:29:33,717 --> 00:29:38,124
Chad G. P. Taylor: Yes. For our final
token, set both selectors to No One and

543
00:29:38,124 --> 00:29:41,430
Chad G. P. Taylor: all three switches off
while perpetual distribution is still

544
00:29:41,430 --> 00:29:43,267
Chad G. P. Taylor: enabled. Then turn it
off.

545
00:29:43,467 --> 00:29:47,353
Ewa Dashwood: Lock first, hide second,
just like the default mint

546
00:29:47,353 --> 00:29:48,217
Ewa Dashwood: destination rule.

547
00:29:48,417 --> 00:29:51,267
Chad G. P. Taylor: Exactly. The hidden
settings still matter.

548
00:29:51,267 --> 00:29:54,054
Chad G. P. Taylor: With perpetual
distribution off, enable Pre-Programmed

549
00:29:54,054 --> 00:29:58,699
Chad G. P. Taylor: Distribution. Enter one
day, zero hours, zero minutes, a public

550
00:29:58,699 --> 00:30:03,809
Chad G. P. Taylor: recipient identity, and
amount two hundred fifty: two and a half

551
00:30:03,809 --> 00:30:05,667
Chad G. P. Taylor: tokens at two decimals.

552
00:30:05,867 --> 00:30:09,717
Ewa Dashwood: Can I add another allocation
for two days and one hundred base units?

553
00:30:09,917 --> 00:30:13,835
Chad G. P. Taylor: Use Add New
Distribution Entry, then Remove it after

554
00:30:13,835 --> 00:30:17,319
Chad G. P. Taylor: the example. Distinct
time or recipient matters; identical

555
00:30:17,319 --> 00:30:20,367
Chad G. P. Taylor: timestamp-and-identity
keys can overwrite an earlier amount.

556
00:30:20,567 --> 00:30:24,167
Ewa Dashwood: Does one day mean exactly a
day after registration completes?

557
00:30:24,367 --> 00:30:27,983
Chad G. P. Taylor: The timestamp is
calculated when the form parses, and

558
00:30:27,983 --> 00:30:31,600
Chad G. P. Taylor: reopening preview can
recalculate it. The recipient later uses

559
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:34,413
Chad G. P. Taylor: Claim. Remove practice
entries and disable this

560
00:30:34,413 --> 00:30:35,217
Chad G. P. Taylor: feature afterward.

561
00:30:35,233 --> 00:30:38,662
Ewa Dashwood: Would Once Per Identity
Distribution make a giveaway limited to

562
00:30:38,662 --> 00:30:40,033
Ewa Dashwood: one claim per person?

563
00:30:40,233 --> 00:30:45,116
Chad G. P. Taylor: Per identity, not per
person. A user can control several

564
00:30:45,116 --> 00:30:48,533
Chad G. P. Taylor: identities. This
feature isn't proof of personhood.

565
00:30:48,733 --> 00:30:50,233
Ewa Dashwood: When can we configure it?

566
00:30:50,433 --> 00:30:54,302
Chad G. P. Taylor: This build requires a
known supported protocol version of at

567
00:30:54,302 --> 00:30:57,398
Chad G. P. Taylor: least fourteen. If
unavailable, we'll show the disabled

568
00:30:57,398 --> 00:31:00,493
Chad G. P. Taylor: explanation and an
instructional callout rather than pretend

569
00:31:00,493 --> 00:31:04,362
Chad G. P. Taylor: it's enabled. Amount
one hundred means one token at two

570
00:31:04,362 --> 00:31:06,683
Chad G. P. Taylor: decimals when an
eligible identity claims.

571
00:31:06,883 --> 00:31:09,783
Ewa Dashwood: Is that claim guaranteed
regardless of supply?

572
00:31:09,983 --> 00:31:13,493
Chad G. P. Taylor: Available supply can
still limit claims. We'll leave the

573
00:31:13,493 --> 00:31:15,833
Chad G. P. Taylor: feature off for our
final registration.

574
00:31:15,833 --> 00:31:19,913
Ewa Dashwood: Before moving on, how do we
distinguish our experiments from the token

575
00:31:19,913 --> 00:31:20,933
Ewa Dashwood: we'll actually publish?

576
00:31:21,133 --> 00:31:24,187
Chad G. P. Taylor: Turn all three
distribution systems off, keep their

577
00:31:24,187 --> 00:31:27,622
Chad G. P. Taylor: change rules locked,
remove the practice group and its

578
00:31:27,622 --> 00:31:31,057
Chad G. P. Taylor: references, and restore
the final owner-based rules. Collapsing a

579
00:31:31,057 --> 00:31:32,583
Chad G. P. Taylor: section isn't disabling
it.

580
00:31:32,783 --> 00:31:36,283
Ewa Dashwood: CloudPoint should have one
thousand initially, a permanent

581
00:31:36,283 --> 00:31:39,783
Ewa Dashwood: ten-thousand ceiling, two
decimals, and ordinary transfers enabled?

582
00:31:39,983 --> 00:31:45,292
Chad G. P. Taylor: Yes. The owner may mint
within the cap and burn its own balance.

583
00:31:45,292 --> 00:31:48,967
Chad G. P. Taylor: Freeze, unfreeze, and
destruction of frozen funds stay disabled.

584
00:31:48,967 --> 00:31:52,233
Chad G. P. Taylor: Those eight emission
recipes were examples, not eight

585
00:31:52,233 --> 00:31:55,500
Chad G. P. Taylor: registered tokens or
completed claims. We'll inspect every

586
00:31:55,500 --> 00:31:57,133
Chad G. P. Taylor: authority again in
JSON.

587
00:31:57,133 --> 00:32:00,803
Ewa Dashwood: Would Document Schemas
create documents automatically or let me

588
00:32:00,803 --> 00:32:02,433
Ewa Dashwood: upload a token logo?

589
00:32:02,633 --> 00:32:06,736
Chad G. P. Taylor: Neither. It adds
document types to the same data contract,

